1. Introduction

This project demonstrates a Secure Coding Review of a deliberately vulnerable Python Flask application.

The purpose was to identify security vulnerabilities using:

Manual source-code inspection
Bandit v1.9.4 for Python SAST
Semgrep v1.171.0 using the auto ruleset

After identifying the vulnerabilities, security fixes were implemented in a separate file named app_fixed.py. The corrected application was then rescanned to measure the effectiveness of the remediation.

The workflow followed was:

Vulnerable Code → SAST Scan → Vulnerability Assessment → Remediation → Post-Remediation Scan

2. Methodology

The original app.py was intentionally created with multiple security weaknesses representing common vulnerabilities found in web applications.

The following steps were performed:

Review the source code manually.
Run Bandit against app.py.
Run Semgrep against app.py.
Record the security findings.
Map vulnerabilities to their corresponding CWE categories.
Create app_fixed.py.
Apply secure coding practices.
Run Bandit against app_fixed.py.
Run Semgrep against app_fixed.py.
Compare the before/after results.
Manually review residual SAST findings.
3. Tools Used
Bandit

Version: 1.9.4

Bandit is a Python static application security testing tool designed to identify common security issues in Python source code.

Command used:

bandit app.py

For saving the results:

bandit app.py -f txt -o reports\bandit_before.txt

After remediation:

bandit app_fixed.py -f txt -o reports\bandit_after.txt
Semgrep

Version: 1.171.0

Semgrep was used with its automatic ruleset:

semgrep scan --config=auto app.py

The results were exported as JSON because Windows' CP1252 encoding caused an error when Semgrep attempted to write its Unicode-rich text output:

semgrep scan --config=auto app.py --json --output reports\semgrep_before.json

After remediation:

semgrep scan --config=auto app_fixed.py --json --output reports\semgrep_after.json
