The Secure Coding Review successfully demonstrated a complete SAST-based security assessment and remediation workflow.

The vulnerable Flask application contained 12 intentionally introduced vulnerability classes, including:

SQL Injection
OS Command Injection
Code Injection
Insecure Deserialization
Weak Password Hashing
Insecure Randomness
SSRF
TLS Certificate Validation Issues
Reflected XSS
Path Traversal
Hardcoded Secrets
Insecure Debug Configuration

After remediation:

Bandit:

15 findings → 3 findings
80% overall reduction

More importantly:

High:    5 → 0   (100% reduction)
Medium:  5 → 0   (100% reduction)

Semgrep:

27 findings → 4 findings
85.2% reduction

The remaining findings were not hidden or falsely marked as resolved. They were manually assessed and documented, demonstrating that SAST tools can produce conservative findings that require human security review.

Overall, the project demonstrates the importance of combining automated security analysis, manual code review, secure coding practices, remediation, and post-remediation verification.
